WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.626 --> 00:00:10.750
KATHERINE MILLER: Hi, Rami.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.940
I hope you're enjoying
the last day here

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.630
of Google Cloud Next 2018.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:15.380
RAMI SHALOM: I am.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.290
KATHERINE MILLER: I'm
really excited to learn

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.825
from you about Stackdriver
Transparent SLI Monitoring.

00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:21.700
Can you tell me a little
bit about this demo?

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:22.699
RAMI SHALOM: Absolutely.

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:25.810
So what we're demoing here is
one of the capabilities of one

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.620
of the products in
Stackdriver Suite.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.220
And what we're doing
here, actually,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.510
is enabling developers
and operational people

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.560
to monitor their service that
they're getting from Google.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.150
So for instance, if your
application is involving

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.930
multiple pieces inside
Google Cloud-- say Spanner,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.450
or Bigtable, or BigQuery--

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.619
every API call that you
are making to Google

00:00:48.619 --> 00:00:51.160
is something that we measure,
and we give you the information

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.610
about what we see.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.420
So in that case, if
something goes wrong

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.130
and you need something
that will enable

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.990
you to know what's
going on, we're

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.870
going to give you the
Google side of things.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.800
So if something
on the Google side

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.810
is malfunctioning
or is misbehaving,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.230
we'll be transparent
and give you

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.314
the information about how
Google is treating you.

00:01:09.314 --> 00:01:10.480
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.830
And so I think you might have
answered my next question.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.910
But what is it that makes
this helpful and useful

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:16.640
to developers?

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.140
RAMI SHALOM: Oh, that's
actually a great question.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.598
So think about the
fact that if you

00:01:19.598 --> 00:01:22.660
have an application that you
own, and your application is

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.270
composed of your code as
an application server,

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.280
but it relies on
services from Google

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.650
and it makes API calls
to certain pieces

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.350
of the infrastructure,
you would want

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.770
to know, first and foremost,
when something happens.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.780
Say you have a long
latency, or you

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.390
have a lot of error messages,
or your customer is complaining

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.680
about something, you want to be
able to pinpoint the right area

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.490
where the problem is.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.940
And so we give you the
indication how the underlying

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.060
services of Google are behaving,
and if this is the case,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.000
get to know where to
focus your attention

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.700
first-- get in touch with Google
to fix the problem that we have

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.730
on our side, or focus on your
application which is actually

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:04.317
misbehaving, and the APIs are
actually behaving correctly.

00:02:04.317 --> 00:02:05.650
KATHERINE MILLER: OK, excellent.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.060
Well, thank you so
much for sharing

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.339
a little bit about the demo,
and enjoy the rest of the show.

00:02:09.339 --> 00:02:10.297
RAMI SHALOM: Thank you.

00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:13.289
[MUSIC PLAYING]

